   Transfer, Hefei, PEOPLES R CHINA, MAY 24-25, 2012},
Organization = {Hefei Univ; Univ Rostock; State Univ N Carolina; Hefei Assoc Sci \&
   Technol; Anhui Environm Sci Soc},
Abstract = {Concerns have been raised about potential adverse effects of silver
   nanoparticles (AgNPs) on human health and the environment, as the widely
   use leads to AgNPs release into the environment. It is necessary to find
   out if traditional water treatment processes can effectively remove
   AgNPs. In this study, the removal of AgNPs by the coagulation process
   using aluminum sulfate was investigated. The coagulation conditions,
   including the dosages of the coagulants and solution pH, were optimized
   based on the removal efficiency of AgNPs. Results showed that AgNPs
   could be effectively removed at pH 5 similar to 8, and the best removal
   efficiency was 97.3\% with 495mg/L aluminum sulfate at p117.5.
   Transmission electron microscope image and X-ray diffraction spectra
   showed the, formation of AgNPs in the coagulation flocs, indicating the
   coagulation was the major AgNPs removal mechanism. The results of this
   study will contribute to further research on the reduction of the risk
   of nanoparticles in the environment:},
