   NY, SEP 11-13, 2005},
Organization = {New York Acad Sci},
Abstract = {This article presents initial data on the application of a pain protocol
   in posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD) to assess the neural circuitry
   that is involved in the disorder, with a special interest in the role of
   the anterior cingulate cortex (ACC). Preliminary analysis demonstrated a
   specific and differentiated role for the ACC. Fixed affects analysis
   demonstrated that stimuli subjectively perceived as more painful
   resulted in increased activation of the caudal ACC, whereas less painful
   stimuli resulted in increased activation of the rostral ACC in both
   groups.},
